<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vistula Lagoon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vistula Bay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> Lagoon is a brackish lagoon on the southern Baltic coast, separated from the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> by the Vistula Spit and now divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The Prussian lands of Pogesania and Warmia lay along its shores before the Teutonic conquest of the 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon.jpg" alt="Vistula Lagoon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vistula_Lagoon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Vistula Lagoon is a brackish lagoon on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> roughly 90 kilometres long, 10 to 24 kilometres wide and up to 5 metres deep, separated from the Gdańsk Bay by the Vistula Spit. It is the mouth of a few branches of the Vistula River, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogat">Nogat</a> and Szkarpawa, and of the Pregolya River. The Kursenieki, a nearly extinct ethnic group, lived on the Vistula Spit by the lagoon, and the surrounding area was inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, Germans and Kursenieki.</p><p>The earliest version of its name was recorded by Wulfstan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a> at the end of the 9th century as Estmere, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> rendition of the Old Prussian Aīstinmari, meaning the lagoon or sea of the Aesti. In the first half of the 13th century deeds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> call it in Latin Mare Recens, fresh sea, in contrast to the Baltic, the Mare Salsum. By 1288 the form Recenti Mari Hab corresponds with the German Frisches Haff. Its Russian name since 1946 is Kaliningradskiy zaliv, Kaliningrad Bay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the first half of the 13th century the lagoon was part of the State of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>. In 1454 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> incorporated the region into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> at the request of the anti-Teutonic Prussian Confederation. After the peace treaty of 1466 the western part belonged to the Polish provinces of Royal Prussia and Greater Poland, and the eastern part was a Polish fief held by the Teutonic Knights and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Ducal Prussia</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> the territory formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>, which became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871. From 1920 to 1939 it was split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the Free City of Danzig. In 1945 the western part, 43.8 percent, became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the eastern part fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. That winter refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> crossed the frozen lagoon on their way west after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> reached its coast near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a> on 26 January. Attacked by Soviet aircraft, thousands were killed or fell through the broken ice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The lagoon is divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbląg</a>, Tolkmicko, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frombork">Frombork</a> and Krynica Morska, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Kaliningrad Oblast, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Kaliningrad</a>, Baltiysk and Primorsk. Kaliningrad and Baltiysk are major seaports on it. It is an Important Bird Area of Poland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vistula Lagoon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pogesania lay along the <b>Vistula Lagoon</b> east of the Elbląg river, in modern northern Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/pogesanians">Pogesanians</a></li><li><blockquote>The Warmians lived in Warmia, south-east of the <b>Vistula Lagoon</b> in today's northern Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/warmians">Warmians</a></li></ul></section>
